PART 2 — MULTI-STRENGTH / MULTI-COLUMN COMPOSITION (EI + RS)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

BUG: Vasopressin (EI), Carboprost (EI), Chlorpromazine (RS) all have MULTIPLE 
quantity columns in the Excel and produce wrong values. The parser mishandles 
multi-column composition.

Multi-strength = one Excel with several quantity columns:
   Carboprost:   "Quantity per mL" | "Quantity per 1mL"   (pre-filled syringe)
   Chlorpromazine: "Qty./1mL" | "Qty./2mL"                 (25mg/mL & 50mg/2mL)
   Vasopressin:  "Quantity/mL" | "Quantity/Vial (100 mL)"  (unit-dosed)
   Generic OSD:  "10 mg" | "20 mg" | "40 mg" columns

Fix in composition parser (EI and RS share this logic):

1. Detect ALL quantity columns, not just the first. A quantity column = header 
   matching any of (case-insensitive, fuzzy):
   "qty", "quantity", "amount", "mg/", "/ml", "/mL", "/vial", "/tab", 
   "/capsule", "/unit", "per ml", "per vial", "per unit", "per 1ml", 
   "per 2ml", strength patterns like "^\d+\s*mg$".
   Store each as a strength option with its label and per-component values.

2. SELECT exactly ONE strength column for the assessment (never sum multiple):
   Selection rule (see Part 3 for the derivation):
     selected_strength = MDD_value ÷ doses_per_day   (implied strength)
     Match to the nearest strength column by API quantity.
     If no column matches within tolerance → warn, fall back to the column 
     whose API qty × doses gives the highest drug-product mass (true 
     worst-case), and log which column was chosen and why.

3. Once one column is selected, every downstream calc uses ONLY that column's 
   per-component quantities. The other columns are ignored for math (retained 
   in Table 1 display for reference).

4. Table 1 (composition) may DISPLAY all strength columns (as approved docs 
   do). But Table 3/4 (EI) and Section 5 (RS) use only the selected strength.

Chlorpromazine RS worked example (MDD 2400 mg, mdd_unit mg):
   API concentration = 25 mg/mL (same in both 1mL and 2mL columns → 
   25mg/1mL and 50mg/2mL are the SAME concentration).
   daily_volume = MDD ÷ concentration = 2400 ÷ 25 = 96 mL/day
   Select either column (same concentration); per-mL basis:
     Chlorpromazine: 25 mg/mL × 96 = 2400 mg ✓
     Ascorbic acid:  2 × 96 = 192 mg
     Na Metabisulfite: 1 × 96 = 96 mg
     Na sulfite: 1 × 96 = 96 mg
     Na chloride: 6 × 96 = 576 mg
     Water/Nitrogen: q.s. → NA

Carboprost EI worked example (MDD 7.25 mg, salt/base footnote present):
   "# 332 mcg Carboprost tromethamine ≡ 250 mcg carboprost" → salt/base.
   Two columns "Quantity per mL" and "Quantity per 1mL" appear identical 
   (same values) — treat as ONE strength (250 mcg/mL). If truly duplicate 
   columns, dedup them; don't double-count.
   Salt handling: strength label is base (250 mcg) but formulation weight is 
   salt (332 mcg). Multiplier from MDD ÷ base; component amount uses salt 
   weight (same salt/base rule as Trilaciclib).

Acceptance:
   Carboprost EI, Chlorpromazine RS, Vasopressin EI all parse the multi-column 
   Excel, select ONE strength, and produce correct daily exposures.

